    {
      "ordinal": 5,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "2.2",
      "title": "Eye bars Description",
      "body": "Eye bars can be:\n\n● Externally Riveted Eye bars This type of Eye bars is documented by historical sources for specific helmets. Therefore, they are only allowed on helmet models that can be historically referenced to have externally riveted Eye bars. If there is no supporting evidence for a particular helmet style, externally riveted Eye bars are not permitted on that helmet.\n\n● Integrated Eye bars This type of Eye bars is documented by historical sources for certain helmets as part of the visor or faceplate itself. They are only permitted on helmet models that can be shown to have used integrated Eye bars historically. If no suitable source exists, integrated Eye bars are not allowed.\nThey must closely replicate the shape, placement, and size seen in surviving artifacts or credible period art.\n\n● Safety Eye bars This type of Eye bar is not documented by historical sources. These concealed Eye bars are allowed solely to increase safety in modern buhurt combat. They typically consist of metal bars riveted or welded to the inside of a helmet, thus remaining concealed from the outside.\n\nNumber and Placement of Safety Eye bars:\n\n○ For helmets with two separate Eye slots, you may install up to two (2) vertical Eye bars per Eye slot.\n\n○ For helmets with one continuous Eye slot, you may install up to five (5) Eye bars across the slot.",
